          "id": "event-113",
          "title": "Metadata Matters: Perceptions on the Implementation of Darwin Core and Other Considerations in the Biocollections Cyberinfrastructure",
          "start": "2:00 PM",
          "end": "2:15 PM",
          "speakers": [
            "Bradley Wade Bishop"
          ],
          "abstract": "Bio-and geo-collections inform scientific discovery, advance knowledge, influence policy decisions, and serve as educational resources. Aggregating biocollections data requires several steps from curation of physical items, data capture and digitization, and creating DarwinCore (DwC) compliant metadata to allow for discoverability in institutional, discipline-specific, and data repositories such as GBIF. With differing curation practices over time, technological and methodological advancements, and evolving funding priorities, the physical research objects, their associated metadata, and derived data and media create a complicated cyberinfrastructure. This survey of curators and collection managers was conducted to better understand their perspectives on the data curation of biocollections. The survey focused on the characteristics of collections professionals, their data storage, access, and reuse practices, challenges to DwC metadata implementation, and who their anticipated users are. Recruitment through TDWG, iDigBio, SPNHC, and discipline-specific listservs led to 60 usable responses. Half of respondents held doctoral degrees, had been in their roles for more than 10 years, and 75% worked at academic institutions. Many themes emerged in relation to perceived roadblocks when mapping to data standards, such as data complexity and representing linked or derived data, gaps in standards development and community implementation guidelines as well as discipline-specific data practices, capacity and technical challenges, and a need for continued support and education around implementing standards and communicating changes or new terminology. The results can inform the building of a robot-ready cyberinfrastructure to increase data access and reuse.",
