      "items": [
        {
          "id": "event-149",
          "title": "How is digitisation doing? Monitoring the increase in digitised collections using MIDS",
          "start": "4:00 PM",
          "end": "4:15 PM",
          "speakers": [
            "Elspeth Haston"
          ],
          "abstract": "The Minimum Information about a Digital Specimen (MIDS) standard has been under development by the TDWG MIDS task group for several years and entered expert review in early 2026. It provides a framework for automatically and reproducibly assessing the digitisation level achieved by a specimen, based on its provided data properties. Applying the MIDS level calculation to historical and recent GBIF specimen data snapshots, we shed light on the growth of digitised specimen collections in recent years and investigate patterns across the dimensions of taxonomy, preservation type, location, and age.",
          "virtual": false,
          "cancelled": false,
          "note": "",
          "sessionCode": "SYM22",
          "sessionTitle": "State of the Collections Description Interest Group. What's Happening and What's Not!",
          "room": "SAL C",
          "day": "Tuesday 22 September",
          "track": "COLLECTIONS DIGITISATION & MANAGEMENT"
        },
        {
          "id": "event-150",
          "title": "The Latimer Core standard and the Global Registry of Scientific Collections",
          "start": "4:15 PM",
          "end": "4:30 PM",
          "speakers": [
            "Marie Grosjean"
          ],
          "abstract": "The Global Registry of Scientific Collections (GRSciColl) aims to catalogue physical scientific collections, their content, location, associated staff and institutions. GRSciColl was originally developed before the Latimer Core (LtC) Standard became ratified. It has its own model, terms and associated vocabularies. The information can be stored in different types of entities: Institution and collection entries, which contain a lot of textual descriptions. Specimen occurrence records shared on the Global Biodiversity Information Facility (GBIF) platform and linked to GRSciColl institution and collection entries. Semi-structured tables called “collection descriptor groups”. They are used to describe collection content and are associated with collection entries. In this presentation, we will show where and how GRSciColl is integrating the LtC standard: GRSciColl entries can be accessed as well as uploaded in the LtC standard. GRSciColl collection descriptors groups use the LtC terms and suggested vocabularies. The objectClassificationName term is particularly useful when capturing information about subcollections corresponding to polyphyletic groups. It allows for example to find collections containing algae: https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/collection/search? objectClassification=Algae The use of LtC standard in training material for modelling of collection data.\n\nWe will also highlight some of the challenges of this integration.",
          "virtual": true,
          "cancelled": false,
          "note": "",
          "sessionCode": "SYM22",
          "sessionTitle": "State of the Collections Description Interest Group. What's Happening and What's Not!",
          "room": "SAL C",
          "day": "Tuesday 22 September",
          "track": "COLLECTIONS DIGITISATION & MANAGEMENT"
        },
        {
          "id": "event-151",
          "title": "Once and Future Latimer Core: Status update and open conversation.",
          "start": "4:30 PM",
          "end": "4:45 PM",
          "speakers": [
            "Sharon Grant"
          ],
          "abstract": "Latimer Core (LtC) is the TDWG standard intended to support description and exchange of information about collections and their associated organizational, and preservation contexts. Over the past year, the Maintenance Group has focused on improving its conceptual model, refining documentation, and exploring practical serialization and interoperability patterns that help long-term sustainability and implementation across diverse collection-management environments.\n\nThis presentation will provide an overview of the current state of LtC, summarize the work undertaken during 2025-2026, and introduce some implementation examples, including draft JSON and RDF/XML exchange formats. Together, these examples aim to illustrate both the flexibility of the LtC conceptual model and the unresolved questions surrounding structure, identifiers, and semantic interoperability.\n\nThe presenters will invite discussion of larger questions that extend to other TDWG efforts: How do we make standards understandable and maintainable for people across different domains of expertise? How should TDWG approach controlled vocabularies and governance? How should we model relationships between terms, and structure their identifiers (IRIs)? How do we document dataset creation, generation, or AI-assisted workflows? What happens when our standards depend on concepts borrowed from outside the TDWG ecosystem?\n\nThis session embraces the messier reality of standards work: balancing flexibility and structure, interoperability and usability, technical rigor and human comprehension. The goal is to share where LtC stands today, and invite discussion. How can TDWG standards remain useful, maintainable, and adaptable as biodiversity data practices continue to evolve?",
          "virtual": false,
          "cancelled": false,
          "note": "",
          "sessionCode": "SYM22",
          "sessionTitle": "State of the Collections Description Interest Group. What's Happening and What's Not!",
          "room": "SAL C",
          "day": "Tuesday 22 September",
          "track": "COLLECTIONS DIGITISATION & MANAGEMENT"
        },
        {
          "id": "event-152",
          "title": "Assessing record quality and completeness in geoscience collections",
          "start": "4:45 PM",
          "end": "5:00 PM",
          "speakers": [
            "Adam Mansur"
          ],
          "abstract": "The Minimum Information about a Digital Specimen (MIDS) standard allows collections to assess digitization progress using a numeric, discipline-specific score. Here we describe efforts to assess this type of data quality score in collections of geological specimens at the Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History (NMNH). We examine MIDS scores for subsets of the collection and compare them to established, internal record quality scores from the Smithsonian's Collections and Digitization Reporting System (CDRS). Our goal is to determine how to best implement MIDS, while also reviewing potential updates to CDRS. MIDS scores can range from 0 (Bare) to 3 (Extended). Most records from the geology collections score 1 (Basic) or 2 (Intermediate). No records score above 2. Low scores largely reflect 1) a lack of detailed metadata for older specimens and 2) incomplete data from ongoing digitization projects. Certain subcollections have more complete data but recently acquired collections score similarly to older collections. CDRS scores are calculated in the collections database using discipline-specific rulesets. Like MIDS, CDRS assesses digitization progress, but it differs from MIDS in four key ways: 1) it includes elements for physical collections management, like storage location and accession status; 2) it excludes some properties that appear in MIDS; 3) it may include data quality checks, which were omitted from MIDS by design; and 4) inconsistent rulesets make comparisons between disciplines difficult. We expect MIDS to be most useful for improving the completeness of published datasets and allowing comparisons between organizations, whereas CDRS scores will better capture internal data quality and collections management concerns.",
          "virtual": false,
          "cancelled": false,
          "note": "",
          "sessionCode": "SYM22",
          "sessionTitle": "State of the Collections Description Interest Group. What's Happening and What's Not!",
          "room": "SAL C",
          "day": "Tuesday 22 September",
          "track": "COLLECTIONS DIGITISATION & MANAGEMENT"
        }
      ]
