          "speakers": [
            "Steen Dupont"
          ],
          "abstract": "As agentic AI systems become more capable, they offer new opportunities for interacting with complex digital infrastructures, specialist literature and collections data. These systems can move beyond single-purpose language models or fixed Retrieval-Augmented Generation workflows by supporting more flexible, context-aware interactions with knowledge. However, highly versatile agentic systems also raise important concerns around cyber security, governance, compute requirements and token costs, particularly when deployed at institutional scale.\n\nThis presentation explores the use of local LLMs and agentic workflows for the analysis of scientific literature and the extraction of structured data relevant to research and museum collections. Operating on local hardware, the system is designed to ingest, interpret and curate knowledge from research papers, reports and archival documents, linking extracted information to existing collections records and domain knowledge.\n\nUsing Dell GB10 systems and high-performance devices, we demonstrate how contained LLM workflows can identify taxonomic, morphological, geographic and ecological information within text, converting narrative scientific knowledge into structured, machine-readable data. This enables the enrichment of collections databases and supports the integration of external knowledge sources into digitisation workflows.\n\nBy operating within a local/on-device environment, the approach maintains control over data processing. Live demonstrations will show how local LLMs and agentic systems can augment research and curation workflows by helping researchers and curators unlock information contained within scientific literature and integrate it directly into research and collections-based work.",
          "virtual": false,
